title: NetScript — the Deno meta-framework for typed, observable systems
---

{{ comp.hero({
  tagline: "Define the boundary once. NetScript carries it through the stack.",
  subhead: "NetScript is a meta-framework for Deno. It composes Fresh, Hono, oRPC, and .NET Aspire into one workspace, where a typed contract flows from schema to service to rendered page — and the whole system runs as one observed resource graph.",
  ctas: [
    { label: "Start a workspace", href: "/quickstart/", primary: true },
    { label: "Why NetScript", href: "/why/" }
  ]
}) }}

<p class="ns-lede">
Frameworks can supply the web layer, the service layer, and the orchestrator while leaving their
boundaries to the application. Those boundaries are where request types diverge, where durable
work is reduced to local retry loops, and where incident evidence splits across disconnected logs,
resource state, and traces. NetScript gives an operation one contract definition, then uses it to
type the handler and the client and to generate the OpenAPI description. What is derived from the
contract cannot drift from it.
</p>

{{ comp.featureGrid({ items: [
  {
    title: "The contract is the spine",
    body: "A zod schema and an oRPC route define the operation once. A service handler supplies the business logic with contract-locked input and output types; OpenAPI and the SDK client are derived from the same definition, so the boundary shape is never declared twice.",
    href: "/explanation/contracts/"
  },
  {
    title: "The workspace is the unit",
    body: "Apps, services, databases, cache, and background processors form one generated Aspire graph. One command starts it; one dashboard carries its health, logs, and traces.",
    href: "/orchestration-runtime/"
  },
  {
    title: "Runtimes for work that outlives a request",
    body: "Workers, sagas, triggers, and streams plug into the same workspace and the same telemetry, so durable work is part of the system rather than a sidecar you integrate yourself.",
    href: "/durable-workflows/"
  }
] }) }}

## One definition, carried end to end

{{ comp.diagram({ src: "/assets/diagrams/contract-flow.svg", alt: "One zod schema and oRPC route feed a hand-written but contract-locked service handler, a generated OpenAPI document, and a derived SDK client with query keys; the SDK feeds the page loader and island, so server and callers cannot drift.", caption: "The principle in one picture: declare the boundary once; write the handler against it; derive the OpenAPI document and client from it; consume those types in the page." }) }}

The same operation at three authored points in the stack. Tab 1 is the only place its input and
output shapes are declared; the handler and page remain application code, but their boundary types
come from that contract.

{{ comp.tabbedCode({ tabs: [
  {
    label: "1 · The contract",
    lang: "ts",
    code: "// contracts/versions/v1/users.contract.ts\nimport { z } from 'zod';\nimport { oc } from '@orpc/contract';\nimport { implement } from '@orpc/server';\n\nexport const UsersListInputSchemaV1 = z.object({\n  limit: z.number().int().positive().max(100).default(20),\n});\nexport const UsersListItemSchemaV1 = z.object({\n  id: z.number().int().positive(),\n  name: z.string().min(1),\n  status: z.enum(['active', 'invited']),\n});\n\n// The contract binds method + input + output. No handler yet.\nexport const UsersContractV1 = {\n  list: oc.route({ method: 'POST' })\n    .input(UsersListInputSchemaV1)\n    .output(z.object({ items: z.array(UsersListItemSchemaV1) })),\n};\n\nexport const UsersV1 = implement(UsersContractV1);"
  },
  {
    label: "2 · The service",
    lang: "ts",
    code: "// services/users/src/routers/v1.ts\nimport { v1 } from '@my-app/contracts';\n\n// `input` is typed from the contract. The return value must\n// satisfy the contract's output schema — or it does not compile.\nexport const UsersV1 = {\n  list: v1.users.list.handler(async ({ input }) => {\n    const items = await listUsers({ limit: input.limit });\n    return { items };\n  }),\n};\n\n// services/users/src/main.ts\n// defineService() serves the router with OpenAPI, typed RPC,\n// health endpoints, and request logging wired in.\nimport { defineService } from '@netscript/service';\nimport { router } from './router.ts';\n\nawait defineService(router, { name: 'users', version: '1.0.0' });"
  },
  {
    label: "3 · The page",
    lang: "tsx",
    code: "// apps/dashboard/routes/users/index.tsx\nimport { definePage } from '@netscript/fresh/builders';\nimport { api } from '@app/lib/api-clients.ts'; // derived from the contract\nimport { routes } from '@app/router.ts';\nimport UsersTable from './(_islands)/UsersTable.tsx';\n\nexport const usersPage = definePage()\n  .withRoute(routes.users.$route)\n  .withLayer('users', UsersTable, {\n    loader: async (ctx) => {\n      // Same contract action and input/output types. The server loader\n      // reads the cache; the island receives the result as layer props.\n      const input = { limit: ctx.search.limit };\n      const entry = await api.users.list.getCachedEntry(input);\n      if (entry) return { users: entry.data, cachedAt: entry.cachedAt };\n      return {\n        users: await api.users.list.queryOptions(input).queryFn(),\n        cachedAt: Date.now(),\n      };\n    },\n  })\n  .withLayout((slots) => <main class='ns-page'>{slots.users()}</main>)\n  .build();\n\nexport const { default: page } = usersPage;\nexport { page as default };"
  }
] }) }}

<p class="ns-lede">
Change the schema in tab 1 and the handler in tab 2 stops compiling, the OpenAPI document updates,
and the loader in tab 3 sees the new types — before anything reaches a browser. That propagation is
the meta-framework: not a bigger library, but ownership of the seams between the libraries you
already know. <a href="{{ '/explanation/contracts/' |> url }}">How the type flow works</a> ·
<a href="{{ '/web-layer/' |> url }}">how pages consume it</a>.
</p>

